WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.683
Quantifiers say who gets chosen first

00:00:02.683 --> 00:00:07.250
For every real number x, there exists a larger real number y

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:14.100
The choice of y may depend on x; it need not be one number that works for everybody.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.531
Choose an arbitrary real x, then define y as x plus one

00:00:20.531 --> 00:00:24.808
Subtracting x leaves the true inequality one greater than zero

00:00:24.808 --> 00:00:30.367
This single argument covers every x, rather than merely checking the displayed examples.

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:33.227
Now reverse the order

00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:37.128
One fixed y would have to exceed every real x

00:00:37.128 --> 00:00:41.809
After that y is chosen, choose x equal to y plus one

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:46.100
The proposed universal inequality fails, so the reversed statement is false.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.653
Negating an all statement requires one counterexample

00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:54.836
Negating a some statement requires that every candidate fail

00:00:54.836 --> 00:01:03.667
Keep the allowed universe fixed: changing from integers to positive integers can change whether the same sentence is true.
